---
import Article from "../layouts/Article.astro";
import { data, fmtDate, whole, orgName, decisionHref } from "../lib/data";
import { decisionPost, shortDate } from "../lib/posts";

const rows = data.decisions ?? [];
const post = decisionPost();
const s = data.suites.find((x) => x.id === "decisions");
const nItems = s ? s.n_public + s.n_private : 0;
const kindLabel = { decision: "Decision model", local: "Local LLM", reference: "Cloud LLM" } as const;
const bestDecision = rows.find((r) => r.kind === "decision");
const bestLocal = rows.find((r) => r.kind === "local");
const showHard = rows.some((r) => r.hard != null);
const unsupported = data.decisions_unsupported ?? [];
const nOpen = decisionOnly().filter((r) => r.open_weights).length;
function decisionOnly() { return rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "decision"); }
const decisionRows = rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "decision");
// Regular models you can run at home that beat the best dedicated decision model.
const beaters = bestDecision ? rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "local" && r.accuracy > bestDecision.accuracy) : [];
const smallestBeater = [...beaters].filter((r) => r.params_b).sort((a, b) => a.params_b! - b.params_b!)[0];
const decHard = decisionRows.map((r) => r.hard).filter((h): h is number => h != null);
const bestLocalHard = Math.max(...rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "local").map((r) => r.hard ?? 0));
const title = "Best Decision Models (Clef, Jev, Microsoft-Decision-1…) vs. Regular LLMs | Model Tested";
const description = post?.dek ?? "Decision models tested on real classification, routing and triage tasks.";
---
<Article
  seoTitle={title}
  description={description}
  heading={post?.title ?? "Decision models"}
  dek="Decision models don't write text. You give them a situation and a few multiple-choice questions, and they return a probability for every option. We tested them, and regular LLMs, on the same real-world calls."
  kicker={`Rankings · updated ${shortDate(data.generated)}`}
  crumbs={[{ href: "/", label: "Home" }, { href: "/topics/rankings/", label: "Rankings" }]}
  cover={post?.cover}
  nav="rankings"
>
  {rows.length === 0 ? <p class="dek">Results are being collected.</p> : (
    <>
      <div class="short">
        <strong>The short version</strong>
        <ul>
          {bestDecision && <li>Best dedicated decision model: <b>{bestDecision.name}</b>, right on {whole(bestDecision.accuracy)}% of calls.</li>}
          {bestLocal && <li>Best regular model you can run at home: <a href={`/models/${bestLocal.slug}/`} style="color: var(--blue)">{bestLocal.name}</a>, {whole(bestLocal.accuracy)}%.</li>}
          {beaters.length > 0 && <li>
            {beaters.length} free models you can run yourself beat every dedicated decision model{smallestBeater && <>, including the small <a href={`/models/${smallestBeater.slug}/`} style="color: var(--blue)">{smallestBeater.name}</a></>}.
          </li>}
          {decHard.length > 0 && <li>The gap shows on the hard cases: decision models got {whole(Math.min(...decHard))}–{whole(Math.max(...decHard))}% of them right; the best regular models got {whole(bestLocalHard)}%.</li>}
          <li>"Calibration" shows how honest a model's confidence is: 100 means it is always right and always sure; low numbers mean it is often confidently wrong.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="decision-models">Best decision models</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: -6px">
        Only the dedicated decision models, ranked. {nOpen} of {decisionOnly().length} have open weights you can download; the rest are
        available only through an API.
      </p>
      <div class="table-box">
        <table style="min-width: 620px">
          <thead><tr><th class="num">#</th><th>Model</th><th>Weights</th><th class="num">Accuracy</th>{showHard && <th class="num">Hard cases</th>}<th class="num">Calibration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {decisionOnly().map((r, i) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="num" style="color: var(--faint)">{i + 1}</td>
                <td><a href={decisionHref(r)!} style="font-weight: 600">{r.name}</a><div class="sub" style="font-size: 13px">{orgName(r.org)}{r.params_b && <> · {r.params_b >= 10 ? Math.round(r.params_b) : Math.round(r.params_b * 10) / 10}B</>}</div></td>
                <td>{r.open_weights ? <>Open<div class="sub" style="font-size: 13px">{r.license ?? ""}</div></> : <span class="sub">Closed (API only)</span>}</td>
                <td class="num"><b>{whole(r.accuracy)}</b></td>
                {showHard && <td class="num">{r.hard == null ? <span class="sub">–</span> : whole(r.hard)}</td>}
                <td class="num">{whole(r.calibration)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
            {unsupported.map((u) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="num" style="color: var(--faint)">–</td>
                <td><b>{u.name}</b><div class="sub" style="font-size: 13px">{orgName(u.openrouter_id.split("/")[0])}</div></td>
                <td>{u.open_weights ? "Open" : <span class="sub">Closed (API only)</span>}</td>
                <td colspan={showHard ? 3 : 2} class="sub">Couldn't take the test: it only answers yes/no questions about a chat transcript.</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="all-models">Decision models vs. regular LLMs</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: -6px">Everyone who took the test, including free models you can run on your own computer.</p>
      <div class="table-box">
        <table style="min-width: 620px">
          <thead><tr><th class="num">#</th><th>Model</th><th>Type</th><th class="num">Accuracy</th>{showHard && <th class="num">Hard cases</th>}<th class="num">Calibration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="num" style="color: var(--faint)">{r.rank}</td>
                <td>{decisionHref(r) ? <a href={decisionHref(r)!} style="font-weight: 600">{r.name}</a> : <b>{r.name}</b>}<div class="sub" style="font-size: 13px"><code>{r.openrouter_id}</code></div></td>
                <td>{kindLabel[r.kind]}</td>
                <td class="num"><b>{whole(r.accuracy)}</b></td>
                {showHard && <td class="num">{r.hard == null ? <span class="sub">–</span> : whole(r.hard)}</td>}
                <td class="num">{whole(r.calibration)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </>
  )}
  {beaters.length > 0 && bestDecision && (
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this means</h2>
      <p>
        Decision models promise fast, cheap, well-calibrated answers to multiple-choice questions, and on the easy cases they deliver:
        most of them get the obvious calls right. Where they fall behind is when the answer depends on reading a rule carefully and doing a
        small calculation, like counting days in a refund window or converting a time zone. The best regular models handle those much better.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're choosing one for real work, test it on your own hardest cases, not your typical ones. And if you can run a model
        yourself, {smallestBeater ? <a href={`/models/${smallestBeater.slug}/`}>{smallestBeater.name}</a> : "a mid-size local model"} is
        a strong, free baseline to beat.
      </p>
    </div>
  )}
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What we test</h2>
    <p>
      {nItems} situations, each with one to three questions: which team should get a support ticket and how urgent it is, whether an
      email is phishing, which tool an assistant should use, what kind of document something is, whether a message contains sensitive
      data, how severe an incident is, and whether a code change needs a security review. Some are tricky on purpose: sarcasm,
      "this is NOT a billing question", criticism that isn't hate speech.
    </p>
    {(data.n_hard_decisions ?? 0) > 0 && <p>
      {data.n_hard_decisions} of the situations are hard cases: the answer follows from rules we spell out (a refund policy, an
      expense limit, a severity matrix, a contract clause), but getting it right means doing date or money math, converting time
      zones, or ignoring a detail that points the wrong way. They are part of the accuracy score and also shown on their own.
    </p>}
    <p>
      Decision models answer through OpenRouter's Decisions API. Regular LLMs get the same situations and options and are asked to
      give a probability for each option as JSON. Accuracy counts how often the most likely option is right; calibration uses the
      Brier score. <a href="/best-local-llms/methodology/">Full technical details</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div slot="tags" class="chips"><a class="chip" href="/topics/rankings/">Rankings</a></div>
</Article>
